course: everyday
lesson: 47
title: Hiking and the outdoors
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Before a walk in the wild you tell someone where you are going and when you will be back, so that if you do not return, people know where to look.
explain: On the walk you stay on the trail: it keeps you from getting lost, keeps you away from cliff edges and hidden holes, and protects the plants.
explain: You carry water, a snack, a warm or waterproof layer and a whistle, because weather in hills and mountains can change fast and it gets colder the higher you climb.
explain: Ticks are tiny animals that wait on long grass and bushes and bite to drink blood; some carry Lyme disease, so a boy hiking in Connecticut wears long trousers tucked into his socks and checks his skin, including behind his knees, in his armpits and in his hair, after the walk.
explain: Wild animals and plants are watched, not touched: you keep your distance from animals, never feed them, and you do not pick or eat berries or mushrooms you do not know.
explain: Two teenagers in Colorado went hiking without telling anyone where; one hurt an ankle, and because nobody knew their route, it took a whole night for rescuers to find them.
explain: A boy in Wales walked to the edge of a cliff for a photo; the ground at the edge crumbled and he fell, and he was rescued by helicopter with a broken leg.
fact: Before a hike you tell someone your route and when you will be back.
fact: Staying on the trail keeps you from getting lost and protects the plants.
fact: It gets colder the higher you climb a mountain.
fact: You should carry water, a snack, an extra layer and a whistle on a hike.
fact: Three loud blasts on a whistle, then a pause, again and again, is a signal that means you need help.
fact: A whistle can be heard much further away than a shout, and blowing it does not tire your voice.
fact: Ticks live in long grass and bushes and bite to feed on blood.
fact: Some ticks carry Lyme disease, which can make people ill.
fact: After walking in long grass you check your skin for ticks, especially in warm, hidden places.
fact: An adult removes a tick with fine tweezers, pulling it straight out, close to the skin.
fact: Wild berries and mushrooms can be poisonous, so you never eat ones you do not know; birds can eat some berries that poison people, and some poisonous mushrooms look very like shop ones.
fact: You should not drink water from streams without treating it first, because even clear, fast water can carry germs you cannot see.
fact: You never feed wild animals and you keep a safe distance from them.
fact: Telling someone your route and return time means help can start looking for you quickly.
fact: Cliff edges can crumble without warning.
exercise: Why should you tell someone your route before a hike? | So people know where to look if you do not come back | So they can meet you at the top | So they can tell you the weather | So that nobody else walks along the same trail on the same day as you
exercise: Why should you stay on the marked trail? | It keeps you from getting lost and away from hidden dangers | Trails are always the shortest and quickest way to the top | Animals are not allowed on trails | It is the only place with shade
exercise: What happens to the temperature as you climb higher up a mountain? | It usually gets colder | It usually gets warmer | It stays exactly the same | It gets warmer at night only
exercise: Why should you pack a warm, waterproof layer on a sunny morning in the mountains? | The weather can change very quickly | Mountains are always rainy | It makes your backpack balanced | Hiking rules say you must carry a coat
exercise: What does a whistle blown three times in a row mean? | I need help | I have found the trail | Time to eat lunch | Everyone keep walking
exercise: Why is a whistle better than shouting when you need help? | It can be heard far away and does not tire your voice | Animals are frightened away by shouting | Shouting is against the rules on trails | A whistle makes the searchers run much faster to find you
exercise: Where do ticks usually wait for an animal or person to pass? | On long grass and low bushes | High up in the tops of tall trees | Only in rivers and ponds | Only in city parks with paths
exercise: Why are some ticks dangerous? | They can carry Lyme disease | They sting like wasps | They lay eggs under the skin | They are poisonous to eat
exercise: How can you make it harder for ticks to reach your skin? | Wear long trousers tucked into your socks | Wear shorts so you can see them | Walk through the longest grass quickly | Rub mud on your legs
exercise: Where should you check for ticks after a hike? | All over, including behind the knees, armpits and hair | Only on your hands | Only on the soles of your feet | Only on your clothes, never on your skin, because ticks cannot bite through
exercise: How should an adult remove a tick? | With fine tweezers, pulling it straight out close to the skin | By burning it with a match | By covering it with butter and waiting for it to let go | By squeezing its body hard with fingers
exercise: You find bright red berries beside the trail. What should you do? | Leave them, because they could be poisonous | Eat one to see if it tastes good | Eat them if you see birds eating them too | Eat only the ones that are fully ripe
exercise: Why should you not drink straight from a clear mountain stream? | It may carry germs you cannot see | Mountain water is too cold to swallow | Clear water has no minerals at all | Stream water makes you more thirsty
exercise: A deer comes close to the trail. What should you do? | Watch it from a distance and do not feed it | Give it some of your sandwich so it learns you are friendly | Walk up slowly to pat it | Chase it so it runs away
test: A girl in Colorado sets off on a hike on a warm, sunny morning in shorts and a T-shirt. What is she missing? | A warmer layer, because the mountain weather can change | Nothing, sunny mornings in the mountains stay sunny all day | A second pair of shoes | Only a bigger snack
test: A boy finds a tick on his leg after playing in the long grass in Wisconsin. What should he do? | Tell an adult so they can remove it with fine tweezers | Pull hard with his fingers and squeeze its body | Burn it off with a hot match | Leave it alone until it drops off by itself, which is the safest way
test: Which is true about ticks? | They are found in long grass and some carry disease | They live only in hot deserts | They only bite pets, never people | They are so big that you always feel them when they bite
test: A family leaves the trail for a shortcut across a steep, rocky slope. Why is this a bad idea? | They could get lost or fall where nobody would look for them | Shortcuts always take longer | Trails are the only places with enough oxygen to breathe safely up high | Rocks are hotter than the trail
test: Why should you never feed a wild animal in a national park? | It can make the animal lose its fear and become dangerous | Wild animals do not eat human food at all | The animal will follow you home and want to live with you as a pet | Feeding animals makes them sleepy
test: A hiker in Scotland gets hurt and can no longer walk. She has a whistle. How should she call for help? | Three loud blasts, then a pause, and repeat | One long blast, once only | Blow softly so she does not scare the animals | Blow it only when she sees someone
test: Two brothers set off on a forest walk without telling anyone. Why is this risky? | Nobody would know where to search if they got lost | They will not be allowed back home | They might meet the wrong friends | The forest is closed to people who do not tell anyone
test: A girl is very thirsty halfway up a trail and sees a fast, clear stream. What is the safest choice? | Drink the water she brought, or treat the stream water first | Drink from the stream, as fast water is always clean | Drink from a still puddle instead | Wait until she reaches home to drink anything
test: A boy finds mushrooms that look like the ones in the shop. What should he do? | Leave them, because some poisonous ones look very similar | Eat them, as they look just like shop ones | Cook them first, as cooking always makes wild mushrooms safe to eat | Eat a small piece to test them
test: going for a hike, who do i tell | Someone, your route and when you're back | Nobody needs to know | Just post it online later, with pics from the top | Only tell the dog
test: found berries on the hike, can i eat em | Not ones you don't know | Yes, if they're red | Yes, if birds eat them | Yes, if they smell sweet
test: stream water looks clean, drink it? | Not without treating it first | Yes, if it's running fast | Yes, if it's cold | Yes, if it looks clear
test: tick on my leg ewww | An adult pulls it out with fine tweezers | Burn it off | Squeeze it with fingers | Leave it, it will drop off by itself in a bit
test: going on a hike, no need to tell anyone where right | Tell an adult your route and when you will be back | Just go, phones always work up in the hills anyway | Tell them after you get home | Only tell your best friend
test: cliff edge would make an amazing photo | Stay well back, edges can crumble | Go right to the edge for the shot | Lean out a little | Sit with your legs over the edge
test: Ryan says u don't need water for a short hike. true? | No, you can get thirsty and ill even on a short walk | Yes, short walks are always easy, you can drink at home after | Yes, streams are always clean | Yes, unless it is summer
test: did u hear a family went hiking without telling anyone and got stuck for 3 days, the rescue helicopter found them | Real lesson: tell someone your route and when you will be back before a hike | Real lesson: helicopters always find lost people in under an hour, wherever they are | It proves families should hike only in cities | It is gossip, so telling someone does not matter
test: my mate says to get a tick off u burn it with a match so it lets go | Wrong; an adult removes a tick with fine tweezers, pulling it straight out | He is right, burning makes it let go | He is right, then squeeze it hard | He is right, or cover it with butter or nail polish so it can't breathe and lets go
test: Ella got a tick and someone's saying she's dirty and has bugs, pass it on | Do not pass it on; ticks bite anyone in long grass, and the rumour is cruel | Pass it on, because ticks prove it | Pass it on, so nobody sits near her | Pass it on, and say her whole family has them and their house is full of bugs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the temperature upon a mountain? | The temperature falls the higher one climbs | The temperature rises the higher one climbs | The temperature is constant at all heights | The temperature is highest at the summit at night
exam: Identify the items recommended to be carried upon a hike. | Water, a snack, an additional layer of clothing and a whistle | A phone alone, since it renders all other items wholly unnecessary | Heavy books and a radio for entertainment | Nothing, since trails always have shops
exam: It being given that a hiker requires assistance, select the recognised whistle signal. | Three loud blasts | One short blast | Ten soft blasts | A continuous blast lasting one minute
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning the trail. | Remaining upon the trail prevents becoming lost and protects the plants | Leaving the trail is invariably the quickest and safest way to reach the summit | The trail need be followed only when it rains | Trails are of no use to experienced walkers
